@unit UNIT V · DEMOGRAPHY

@chapter 22 | Population I: Theory, Census and Growth

@intro
Demography is the last and one of the most-asked parts of the economy paper. This first chapter covers **population theories** (Malthus, demographic transition, optimum population), the **Census of India** (history, slogans, 2027 Census), **population growth** by decade and its turning points (**1921 and 1951**), and the **population of states and of UP**. All figures are from **Census 2011** unless stated. It contains **{pyqs} PYQs and {ones} one-liners**.
@end

@section 22.1 | Population Theories

@q 16:1-8

@table
Theory | Propounder | Key idea
**Malthusian theory** (1798) | **T.R. Malthus** | Population grows in **geometric**, food in **arithmetic** progression; checks: **preventive** (moral restraint, late marriage) and **positive** (famine, war, disease)
**Optimum population** | **Edwin Cannan** | Size giving the highest per capita income
**Demographic transition** | **W.S. Thompson** (1929), F. Notestein | High birth and death → falling death → falling birth → low both
**Social maladjustment** | **Henry George** | Poverty comes from bad social institutions, not numbers
@end

