@q 16:10

**Natural growth** = **birth rate − death rate**; migration and marriages are not part of it. India is in the **late third stage** of demographic transition: growth is still positive but **clearly slowing** (TFR below replacement since 2020-21). A **stable population** has constant birth and death rates.

@section 22.2 | The Census of India

@q 16:11-12

@src 16:13
@pyq U.P. P.C.S. (Pre) 2023
When was the Census work done for first time in India?
(a) In the year 1871
(b) In the year 1881
(c) In the year 1850
(d) In the year 1861
@ans (b)
@note The first (non-synchronous) census was taken in **1872** under **Lord Mayo**; the first **regular, synchronous** census came in **1881** under **Lord Ripon**. The Commission accepted (a) 1871, which the source calls incorrect; it keys (b).
@end

@src1 16:1
@one U.P. R.O./A.R.O. (Pre) 2023; U.P.P.C.S. (Pre) 2001
The first Census in India during the British period was held in the tenure of
@ans Lord Mayo
@end

@q 16:14-15

@table
Census fact | Detail
First census | **1872** (Lord Mayo); first regular **1881** (Lord Ripon; W.C. Plowden as Census Commissioner)
Basis | **Census Act, 1948**; held every **10 years**; Census is in the **Union List**
**Census 2011** | **15th** census overall, **7th** after independence; slogan "**Our Census, Our Future**"
Census 2021 | Postponed (COVID)
**Census 2027** | **16th** census; first **digital** census with **caste** enumeration; reference date **1 March 2027** (1 October 2026 for snow-bound areas)
Run by | **Registrar General and Census Commissioner**, Ministry of Home Affairs
@end

