@q 16:20-23

@src1 16:3
@one U.P.P.C.S. (Pre) 2005; U.P.P.S.C. (GIC) 2010
The decadal growth rate of population in India was highest during
@ans 1961-1971
@end

@table
Decade | Growth (%) | Note
1901-11 | 5.75 | Slow growth
**1911-21** | **−0.31** | Only fall (influenza, famine) → **1921 = "Year of Great Divide"**
1951-61 | 21.6 | **1951 = demographic divide**: rapid growth begins
**1961-71** | **24.80** | **Highest decadal growth**
1971-81 | 24.66 | **Highest annual exponential growth** (2.22%)
1981-91 | 23.87 | -
1991-2001 | 21.54 | -
**2001-11** | **17.70** | **Lowest since independence**; first decade adding fewer people than the previous one
2011 total | **121.09 crore** | 17.5% of world population on 2.4% of the land
Now | About **146 crore** (UN, 2025) | **India passed China in April 2023** as the most populous country
@end

India's population did grow very fast after **1951**, but the "great divide" year is **1921**, not 1951, so in Q22 the Assertion is true and the Reason false.

